ettings. `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`.claude/settings.local.json`}),` loads from the git repository root instead, even when you start Claude Code in a subdirectory, except in the cases where Claude Code `,_jsx(_components.a,{href:`/en/settings#where-claude-code-looks-for-each-file`,children:`doesn’t use the repository root`}),`, such as on Windows; before v2.1.211, it too loaded only from the current working directory. `,_jsx(_components.a,{href:`/en/agent-sdk/claude-code-features#control-filesystem-settings-with-settingsources`,children:`Agent SDK`}),` sessions load it from the working directory in all versions.`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:`To share that configuration across projects, use one of these approaches:`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`User-level configuration`}),`: place files in `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`~/.claude/agents/`}),`, `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`~/.claude/output-styles/`}),`, or `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`~/.claude/settings.json`}),` to make them available in every project`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Plugins`}),`: package and distribute configuration as a `,_jsx(_components.a,{href:`/en/plugins`,children:`plugin`}),` that teams can install`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Launch from the config directory`}),`: run Claude Code from the directory containing the `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`.claude/`}),` configuration you want`]}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`2`,id:`how-permissions-interact-with-sandboxing`,children:`How permissions interact with sandboxing`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`Permissions and `,_jsx(_components.a,{href:`/en/sandboxing`,children:`sandboxing`}),` are complementary security layers:`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.ul,{children:[`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Permissions`}),` control which tools Claude Code can use and which files or domains it can access. They apply to Bash, Read, Edit, WebFetch, MCP, and every other tool, except that a deny or ask rule can’t block `,_jsx(_components.a,{href:`/en/tools-reference#endconversation-tool-behavior`,children:_jsx(_components.code,{children:`EndConversation`})}),` while any other tool remains.`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.li,{children:[_jsx(_components.strong,{children:`Sandboxing`}),` provides OS-level enforcement that restricts shell commands’ filesystem and network access. It applies only to Bash, PowerShell, and `,_jsx(_components.a,{href:`/en/tools-reference#monitor-tool`,children:`Monitor`}),` commands and their child processes.`]}),`\n`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`Use both for defense-in-depth, since sandbox restrictions still apply even if a prompt injection bypasses Claude’s decision-making. Paths and domains from both sandbox settings and permission rules are `,_jsx(_components.a,{href:`/en/sandboxing#permission-rules`,children:`merged into the final sandbox configuration`}),`.`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`When you enable sandboxing and leave `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`autoAllowBashIfSandboxed`}),` at its default of `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`true`}),`, sandboxed Bash commands run without prompting even if your permissions include a bare `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`Bash`}),` ask rule, or the `,_jsxs(_components.a,{href:`#match-all-uses-of-a-tool`,children:[`equivalent `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`Bash(*)`}),` form`]}),`: the sandbox boundary substitutes for that whole-tool prompt.`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`In `,_jsx(_components.a,{href:`/en/permission-modes#analyze-before-you-edit-with-plan-mode`,children:`plan mode`}),`, Claude Code skips this substitution. Without an ask rule, the `,_jsx(_components.a,{href:`#read-only-commands`,children:`built-in read-only commands`}),` still run without prompting, and