naged tier, and the `,_jsx(_components.a,{href:`/en/managed-settings#managed-only-settings`,children:`keys that only managed settings can set`}),`.`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`One of those keys, `,_jsx(_components.a,{href:`/en/settings-reference#allowmanagedpermissionrulesonly`,children:_jsx(_components.code,{children:`allowManagedPermissionRulesOnly`})}),`, makes managed settings the only settings source of permission rules. Its entry lists every source Claude Code then ignores.`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[_jsx(_components.code,{children:`disableBypassPermissionsMode`}),` is typically placed in managed settings to enforce organizational policy, but it works from any scope. A user can set it in their own settings to lock themselves out of bypass mode.`]}),`\n`,_jsx(Heading,{level:`2`,id:`settings-precedence`,children:`Settings precedence`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`Permission rules follow the same `,_jsx(_components.a,{href:`/en/settings#settings-precedence`,children:`settings precedence`}),` as all other Claude Code settings, with managed settings highest: no other level, including command line arguments, can override a managed permission rule.`]}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`If a tool is denied at any level, no other level can allow it. For example, a managed settings deny can’t be overridden by `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`--allowedTools`}),`, and `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`--disallowedTools`}),` can add restrictions beyond what managed settings define.`]}),`\n`,_jsx(_components.p,{children:`The same holds across settings scopes: if user settings allow a permission and project settings deny it, the deny rule blocks it. The reverse is also true: a user-level deny blocks a project-level allow, because deny rules from any scope are evaluated before allow rules.`}),`\n`,_jsxs(_components.p,{children:[`Embedding hosts can supply additional managed policy via the SDK `,_jsx(_components.code,{children:`managedS